From ff8bd774f963611c45c087dcd45a1011c5c8b5d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rico Rojas <rickrojas@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 20:42:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] perf(dictation): cut hotkey press-to-record latency and
 replay presses dropped during processing

Press to recording start was ~380 ms on macOS, and paste took ~900 ms,
with every press during that window silently dropped. Three causes, one
of them dominant:

- The frontmost-app PID read used `osascript -l JavaScript`, measured at
  350 ms per call. It ran on the press path (the renderer awaited it
  before starting the recording) and again on the paste path (activation
  check, plus retries). macos-text-monitor gains `--frontmost-pid` and
  `--activate <pid>` modes (under 5 ms); textEditMonitor uses them with
  the JXA script kept as the fallback for an older helper binary.
- The renderer awaited the target capture before startRecording. It is
  only consumed at paste time and the main process serializes it, so it
  now runs concurrently with the start.
- Hybrid mode waited the 150 ms push hold delay before starting. Hybrid
  decides tap-vs-hold on release, so it records from the press itself;
  push keeps the delay (resolveHoldStartDelayMs).

A press that lands while the previous transcript is still processing is
now parked and replayed when the lifecycle leaves processing (within
1.5 s; a released push press is discarded), instead of vanishing so the
user presses again and flips the state they expected. Press handlers log
the press, the start offset, and the paste breakdown at debug level.
